From e3dc0fd66d1eb4ab7a6a9ad2fb2cbd958a4c75ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 03:18:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ActiveAE: pause and resume passthrough without losing or
 repeating sound

At a pause Kodi flushes the engine (PAUSESTREAM, FlushEngine()): the
packets sent to the sink and not yet written go back to their pool,
unplayed. For TrueHD, which the engine does not hold to its water level,
that is all it had buffered, 380 to 480 ms; for the others about 200 ms.
The resume starts the sync again as for a new stream (SYNC_START): the
packets that come first are turned into pauses while the error is
averaged, and the error that the lost packets leave is corrected with more
pauses. The sound picks up hundreds of milliseconds further on than where
it stopped, and the receiver gets a stream with a hole in it.

A passthrough stream that the sink packs, alone, playing and whose sync is
measured, is now held instead (HoldRawStream()). The sink gives back what
it had not written (HOLD); the engine keeps copies of those packets and of
the pauses between them, in their order, and the packets go back to the
pool of the stream as before, so that the player feeds it as before. It
also reckons how much the device plays after the picture stopped: all that
it held when the sink answered, and what it played while the pause reached
the engine, from where the video clock last ran (PassthroughPause.h,
NoteClock(), FrozenFor(): a reading of a clock that stood still is not
taken for one that ran).

At the resume (ResumeRawStream()) the copies go back to the sink as they
were, ahead of anything the sync decides from then: the sync decided them
before the pause. The sync goes on in the state it had: in sync; being
corrected, with what was left to correct; being measured, ending with the
average it had (measured on, it would turn into pauses the packets that
the player fed while paused). The sink puts a pause before the copies
(RESUMEGAP), so that what played with the picture stopped is not heard
again and the sound goes on where the picture is; until the sink says what
it holds, the engine counts at least that pause in it. Copies of a pause
before that are still going out are kept again by the next one, and the
copies of a pause are deleted once the sink has given them all back
(ClearDiscardedBuffers()).

A seek or flush, a stream closed and an output opened again let the kept
packets go as before, and tell the sink (HOLDDROPPED), whose silence is
then that of a pause that keeps nothing. An output that is no longer open
or no longer passthrough resumes with the sound kept and the sync as at a
start. Each pause, resume and letting go is said in kodi.log. PCM,
transcoding, a sink that takes the stream unpacked and a stream not yet
measured are as before.
